WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.480
When Neil Armstrong set foot on the Moon in
1969,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.160
600 million people tuned in to watch.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.870
By 1972, enthusiasm had waned a little,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.440
but Apollo 16 carried a significant, if little

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.050
celebrated, first: a telescope to view the

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:21.810
Earth and heavens from the lunar surface.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.280
The chance to put a telescope on the Moon

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.180
was a great opportunity for the young astronomer

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.930
George Carruthers.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.860
With this telescope, he confirmed that gas

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.540
clouds between stars contain molecular

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.630
hydrogen, as astronomers had expected, and

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.140
took photos of Earth in UV light—displaying

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.250
the extent of our hydrogen atmosphere.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:46.480
The Apollo program was wound up later in 1972,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.280
and Carruthers’ telescope has sat lonely on

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.710
the Descartes highlands ever since.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.900
But that didn’t keep scientists from dreaming

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.899
and scheming of using the moon as a base for

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:58.710
observing the universe.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.640
Now, exploring—and exploiting—the Moon

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.539
is back in fashion, and astronomers hope to

00:01:04.539 --> 00:01:06.900
seize this opportunity to turn the moon into

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.299
a grand observatory.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.140
The lonely telescope may soon have some new
friends.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.580
Radio astronomers are particularly eager to

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.340
build a telescope there to peer back into

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.400
the deep, dark past of the universe.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.480
For several hundred million years after the

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.579
big bang had cooled, the universe was dark

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.240
and featureless—filled with neutral hydrogen

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.969
gas, but no stars.

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:36.300
Within those clouds, very occasionally a hydrogen

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.759
atom would spontaneously emit a photon with

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:43.499
a unique frequency of 1.4 gigahertz.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.320
That light is referred to as the 21-cm line,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.899
owing to its wavelength.

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:51.600
The Earth’s ionosphere blocks these faint

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.750
signals from space, and terrestrial transmitters

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.359
cause noisy interference.

00:01:57.359 --> 00:01:59.600
The lunar far side is the perfect place to

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.700
detect these low-frequency signals, as it’s

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.900
shielded from interference from Earth

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.980
and the Moon has no ionosphere.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.420
With an array of widely spaced antennas on

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.440
the far side, astronomers could map the density

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.080
of neutral hydrogen across space and time

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.120
during that dark age, identifying the clumps

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.580
of gas that would later form into galaxies.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.100
Teams of astronomers are planning experiments

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.150
to see just how feasible this is, starting

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:31.340
with moon-orbiting satellites with extending antennas

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.819
designed to detect this dark age signal.

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:37.280
Another plan could use autonomous rovers to

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.810
lay out antennas made of conducting film in

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:44.930
huge arrays to produce a lunar radio observatory.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.560
The rovers could be controlled by crew on

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.069
board NASA’s planned moon-orbiting space station.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:52.880
Although radio astronomy has a compelling

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.159
case for a lunar observatory, optical and

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.020
infrared telescopes on the moon would also

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.760
be hugely valuable.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.200
Telescopes on Earth can be made very large

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.840
to capture the faint light of distant objects,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.500
but they're always at the mercy of shifting

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.629
fluctuations in the density of the atmosphere,

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:13.469
the process that makes stars twinkle.

00:03:13.469 --> 00:03:16.060
Modern telescopes can compensate for this effect

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.299
to a degree with deformable mirrors,

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:22.719
but telescopes in space are immune to the problem.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.300
This is why the Hubble Space Telescope is

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.010
able to peer further than any ground-based

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.639
instrument, despite its relatively small mirror.

00:03:30.639 --> 00:03:33.400
The size of orbiting telescopes like Hubble

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.129
is limited by the capacity of the rockets

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:39.380
that launch them, but if a way could be made

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.030
to make mirrors on the Moon, it might be possible

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.420
to build a telescope with clearer vision than Hubble.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.440
One approach that astronomers have been studying

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:52.080
would be to create a mirror from a spinning liquid.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.200
This process seems straightforward—take a

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.550
large circular dish, fill it with a liquid,

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.180
gently spin it, and centripetal force naturally

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.620
creates the perfect parabolic shape for a mirror.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.700
On Earth, this has been done with liquid mercury,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.740
which is naturally reflective, but on the

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:11.989
moon mercury would evaporate.

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:14.460
Instead, researchers believe they could use

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.829
a molten salt that won’t evaporate; topped

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:19.980
with a thin layer of reflective silver,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.480
less
than 100-nm thick.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.760
In the Moon’s low gravity, the necessary

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.120
support structure would be simple and cheap

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.340
to build, and since the moon is seismically

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:34.639
quiet, no tremors would disturb the spinning liquid.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:37.380
But building a 20-meter telescope on the Moon,

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.520
10 times the width of Hubble, would still

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.820
require transporting over three tonnes of materials

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:47.580
from Earth, which doesn’t come cheap.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.250
One solution to that problem is to use local materials.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.640
One team has made a small test mirror by mixing

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.419
simulated moon dust with epoxy,

00:04:57.419 --> 00:04:59.840
If such telescopes could be constructed cheaply,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.400
then it would be possible to make arrays of

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.900
telescopes and combine their light to achieve

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.517
a resolution unimaginable on Earth.

00:05:07.517 --> 00:05:09.580
Good enough to study the surface of planets

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.380
around nearby stars.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.640
None of these plans have yet gotten the green light,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.200
but with private space agencies and

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.980
companies planning expeditions in the near future,

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.389
opportunities may soon arise.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.620
But renewed interest in the moon comes at a price:

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.419
If companies begin activities such

00:05:28.419 --> 00:05:30.960
as mining for minerals on the lunar surface,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.650
this pristine vantage point may be spoiled

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:59.410
before astronomers even get a chance to use it.

